---
name: std-mcp-server
version: 0.2.0
description: MCP server primitives — mcpServer builder + serveMcp launcher. Wraps @modelcontextprotocol/sdk on JS and io.modelcontextprotocol:sdk on JVM; the interpreter and the native lane implement the protocol directly. Not available on scalajs-spa. Transport.Http (SSE) supported on JS since v0.2.0.
package: std.mcp
exports:
  # Every name this module imports from ./types.ssc is re-exported. The list used to carry only
  # Tool / Transport / requireString, so a consumer had to reach past this module into
  # ./types.ssc for the REST of the same API — and v1's own std/agent-mcp.ssc:26 does not: it
  # imports `Content` from here and failed to resolve it. Types that appear in the signatures
  # below (ToolResult, ResourceResult, PromptResult) are not optional for a caller: you cannot
  # write a handler without naming them. (skip-77-hides-v2-coverage.)
  - McpServer
  - mcpServer
  - serveMcp
  - Tool
  - Resource
  - Prompt
  - Transport
  - Content
  - Message
  - Role
  - ToolResult
  - ResourceResult
  - PromptResult
  - McpError
  - requireString
  - requireInt
  - requireDouble
  - requireBool
  - ElicitationResult
---

# `std.mcp.server`

MCP server API. Same shape as the REST stack: `mcpServer { srv => ... }` registers
handlers; `serveMcp(transport)` starts listening.

Requires `Feature.McpServer` — available on `jvm`, `js` (Node), the interpreter and the native
lane. NOT on `scalajs-spa`, which declares only `Feature.McpClient`.

The interpreter is not a lesser lane here. Driving a `.ssc` server over stdio, it answers
`initialize` with `protocolVersion 2025-06-18` and advertises `tools`, `resources` (with
`subscribe`), `prompts`, `logging` and `completions` — byte-identical capabilities to the native
lane, differing only in `serverInfo` (`ssc-mcp-int` vs `ssc-mcp-native`). Measured, because the
line this replaced said "not available on interpreter" for months and a capability DECLARATION is
not a running server. NOT compared against `jvm`/`js` here: those wrap the vendor SDKs and were not
driven for this note.

```scalascript
[ToolResult, ResourceResult, PromptResult, Content, Message, Role, Transport, McpError,
 Tool, Resource, Prompt, ElicitationResult, requireString, requireInt, requireDouble,
 requireBool](./types.ssc)

// ─── McpServer trait ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Builder object passed to the mcpServer { srv => ... } block.
// Register tools, resources, and prompts before calling serveMcp().
extern class McpServer:
  // Register a tool handler.
  // handler receives JSON-decoded args as Map[String, Any].
  def tool(name: String)(handler: Map[String, Any] => ToolResult): Unit
  def tool(name: String, description: String)(handler: Map[String, Any] => ToolResult): Unit

  // Register a resource handler.
  // handler receives the requested URI as String.
  // `tool` registers `{"type":"object"}` — it accepts anything and validates nothing. Use this when
  // the arguments have a shape worth publishing: the schema reaches the client in `tools/list`, so
  // it is what an agent reads to decide how to call the tool.
  def toolWithSchema(name: String, schema: Map[String, Any])(handler: Map[String, Any] => ToolResult): Unit
  def toolWithSchema(name: String, description: String, schema: Map[String, Any])(handler: Map[String, Any] => ToolResult): Unit

  def resource(uri: String)(handler: String => ResourceResult): Unit
  def resource(uri: String, name: String)(handler: String => ResourceResult): Unit
  def resource(uri: String, name: String, mimeType: String)(handler: String => ResourceResult): Unit

  // Register a prompt handler.
  // handler receives template args as Map[String, Any].
  // One registration serving a FAMILY of uris. `{...}` segments in the template match one path
  // segment each, and the handler receives the CONCRETE uri the client asked for — not the
  // template. Listed via `resources/templates/list`, and used as the `resources/read` fallback when
  // no exact `resource` matches.
  def resourceTemplate(template: String)(handler: String => ResourceResult): Unit
  def resourceTemplate(template: String, name: String)(handler: String => ResourceResult): Unit
  def resourceTemplate(template: String, name: String, description: String)(handler: String => ResourceResult): Unit
  def resourceTemplate(template: String, name: String, description: String, mimeType: String)(handler: String => ResourceResult): Unit

  def prompt(name: String)(handler: Map[String, Any] => PromptResult): Unit
  def prompt(name: String, description: String)(handler: Map[String, Any] => PromptResult): Unit

  // ── Elicitation ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  //
  // Ask the connected client for structured input mid-handler. `schema` is a JSON Schema object;
  // the answer's `action` is "accept", "decline" or "cancel", and `content` is present only for
  // "accept".
  //
  // DECLARED NOW, HAVING BEEN REACHABLE WITHOUT A DECLARATION. The v1 plugin exposes
  // `McpServer.elicit` and builds an undeclared `ElicitationResult` record; nothing in `.ssc` said
  // either existed, so a second lane had no contract to implement against. That is what this
  // declaration is for — see the type in ./types.ssc.
  //
  // Requires the client to have advertised `elicitation` in its initialize capabilities;
  // `clientSupportsElicitation` says whether it did.
  // ONE declaration with a DEFAULT rather than two arities. A v2 plugin member is resolved by
  // `getField(name)` — a value handed back before any argument exists — and every `Value.ClosV`
  // carries a FIXED declared arity, so one name cannot serve two arities there. v1's plugin
  // protocol can (its native matches on the argument list), but the surface has to be expressible
  // on both. `timeoutMs = 0` means "the builder's own default".
  def elicit(message: String, schema: Map[String, Any], timeoutMs: Long = 0): ElicitationResult

  // Lifecycle hooks — called once per client connect / disconnect.
  def onConnected(handler: () => Unit): Unit
  def onDisconnected(handler: () => Unit): Unit

// ─── Factory + launcher ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Configure the MCP server.  Call inside the setup block to register handlers.
// Returns Unit; server state is held by the runtime.
extern def mcpServer(setup: McpServer => Unit): Unit

// Start the MCP server on the given transport.
// Transport.Stdio: blocks until stdin closes.
// Transport.Http(port, path): listens on an HTTP+SSE server (JS only);
//   one SSEServerTransport per client connection; onConnected fires per client.
// Transport.Ws: deferred on all backends.
extern def serveMcp(transport: Transport): Unit
```
